 "notes": [
  "The tutorial's part list matched 'analog sensor' to ntc-temperature-sensor, but the circuit image shows a photoresistor, so the catalogue type 'photoresistor' is used.",
  "Breadboard hole names follow the catalogue convention (bank 'b' = rows f-j, bank 't' = rows a-e). The image draws rows j-f in the upper half, so those holes are named with bank 'b'. Rail hole numbers (tp/tn.N) are approximate positions read from the image; the whole rail is one strip, so only the rail matters electrically.",
  "Circuit: 5V rail -> photoresistor (6-10) -> junction column 10 to A0 and 10k resistor -> GND rail. Pin 9 -> LED anode (col 22) -> LED cathode (col 23) -> 220 ohm resistor -> GND rail. The image puts the LED before the resistor, while the schematic puts the resistor first. The two are electrically equivalent in series, and the image is used.",
  "The LED's longer, bent leg (left, column 22) is taken as the anode, because the blue pin-9 wire joins that column.",
  "Arduino GND.1 was chosen for the GND wire; any GND pin on the power header is equivalent.",
  "Resistor values: r1 = 10k ohm, r2 = 220 ohm (value props in ohms)."
 ],
